    "title": "East of Eden",
    "author": "John Steinbeck",
    "rating": 5,
    "review": "Just about perfect. I have never had a book strike me as immediately as this did. Within the first few pages, certainly in 20 pages, it is clear that it is something truly special and at no point does the story betray that. This is an essential read and I'm a bit ashamed that this took me so long to discover.<br /><br />When I was nearing the end, I started making a plan to make the last few chapters more memorable by reading the ending at some significant spot in Steinbeck's life in New York. I found that he primarily wrote this at 206 E 72nd St in Manhattan. His particular building was long gone, but his former neighbors' townhomes stood proudly modeling to me what his home would have looked like. To speak of his neighbors, they had a nice fence, suitable for leaning on while reading, standing where his front steps may have once been. This was useful because the only spot to sit was the bus stop which seemed to have been a popular place for four-legged creatures to find relief. <br /><br />Putting aside the sappiness of hopping on the subway just to be where a book may or may not have been written, I was skeptical that he actually wrote it there, as he only moved to that location in 1951 and East of Eden was published in 1952. I was thinking he may have only written a few of the last chapters here, or just made some edits. The Penguin Classics edition I read clarified this with letters to his editor at the end of the final chapter:<br /><blockquote>January 29, 1951<br /><br />... and in two days I am moving into the pretty little house on 72nd St. and there I will have a room to work in and I will have love all around me. Maybe I can finally write the book.</blockquote><br /><br />I was feeling better about my excessive sentimentality after reading that while leaning against his neighbor's fence. The next letters chronicle the following 6 months, writing at an incredible pace while thinking he was going too fast or too slow. <br /><br />This book is a wealth of important lessons too numerous to begin to list. The beautiful thing about books like these is that you often think you will get these lessons from parents, grandparents, a wise older brother -- and it just doesn't happen. One can be easily tricked into thinking you should've emerged into adult life having learned the important lessons directly from family, but the truth is that you have to learn them yourself. A book like this will meet you where you are and dispense wisdom you didn't know you needed. No two readers digest the same book because they each needed different lessons to extract from story. There are enough meaningful facts about life in East of Eden that it will soothe whatever it is you face.<br /><br /><blockquote>You're going to pass something down no matter what you do or if you do nothing. Even if you let yourself go fallow, the weeds will grow and the brambles. Something will grow.</blockquote><br /><br />Samuel Hamilton is my favorite character I have discovered in literature so far and I suspect very few will come close. At points he dominates the dialogue and focus and it still isn't enough. Having finished the book, and thinking back to what really struck me while standing in front of a dentist at the site of the author's former home, I recalled:<br /><br /><blockquote>And while I tell you, I am myself sifting my memories, the way men pan the dirt under a barroom floor for the bits of gold dust that fall between the cracks. It’s small mining -- small mining. You're too young a man to be panning memories, Adam. You should be getting yourself some new ones, so that the mining will be richer when you come to age.</blockquote><br /><br />I decided that it was time to walk away. And off to the west there was a just about perfect sunset.",
