    "title": "The Oresteia: Agamemnon, Choephoroe, Eumenides",
    "author": "Aeschylus",
    "rating": 5,
    "review": "In April 1968, RFK famously spoke to a crowd in Indianapolis announcing the assassination of Martin Luther King. My feeling is that it is some of the best spoken word in the history of the English language. So well chosen were RFK’s words (chosen more or less on the spot) that they resonate many decades later and Indianapolis was one of the only major cities in the US that did not riot that night. <br /><blockquote>For those of you who are black and are tempted to fill with hatred and mistrust of the injustice of such an act, against all white people, I would only say that I can also feel in my own heart the same kind of feeling. I had a member of my family killed, but he was killed by a white man.<br /><br />But we have to make an effort in the United States. We have to make an effort to understand, to get beyond, or go beyond these rather difficult times.<br /><br />My favorite poet was Aeschylus. And he once wrote:</blockquote><br /><br />And I will first give you the quote that was shown in my copy, the Everyman’s Library edition translated by George Thomson:<br /><br /><blockquote>He to wisdom leadeth man,<br />He hath stablished firm the law,<br />Man shall learn by suffering.<br />When deep slumber falls, remembered sins<br />Chafe the sore heart with fresh pain, and no<br />Welcome wisdom meets within.<br />Harsh the grace dispensed by powers immortal<br />On the awful bench enthroned.</blockquote><br /><br />I do not know why it is so different, but it makes me want to read the translation quoted by RFK, which is far more beautiful:<br /><br /><blockquote>Even in our sleep, pain which cannot forget<br />falls drop by drop upon the heart,<br />until, in our own despair,<br />against our will,<br />comes wisdom<br />through the awful grace of God. </blockquote><br /><br />The Orestia is a story about the cycle of revenge if sought out, and that wisdom is necessarily predicated by suffering. <br /><br />There is something supremely profound about RFK’s spontaneous recall of this verse given standing in the back of a pickup truck to calm a crowd that the police had already told him they could not protect him from should things turn for the worst. I had never read really any ancient literature before. I imagine that there is something about enduring art like this that can be the only refuge for a brother who witnesses the assassination of his brother, the president. What I learned from being so deeply affected by his speech is that appreciation of literature and any art that speaks to you can land years later. Books we read when fearful or songs that comfort us when depressed — wisdom from suffering — become building blocks for passing along comfort and clarity to others at the right moment.",
