    "title": "War and Peace",
    "author": "Leo Tolstoy",
    "rating": 5,
    "review": "In general I go by: “good things are good” — I feel like people sometimes avoid what is commonly referred to as the best for whatever reason. I think it’s a lot better to just see what all the fuss is about. <br /><br />War and Peace is widely considered one of the greatest novels ever written and it is immediately clear early on into the story. The story just flows so well and while tragic events unfold unceasingly, I’ve never felt more drawn to characters like I was here. <br /><br />In my feeling the book presented a hero composed of two people in Prince Andrew and Pierre Bezhukov. It is difficult to believe in a singular hero, they say to “never meet your heroes” for a reason and even when a novel presents a flawed character who becomes a hero, it feels off. In presenting these two as friends from early on in childhood and how unique that kind of connection is that it can exist across battlefields and years of zero contact, in presenting their flawed decisions and guidance of each other, in presenting their widely divergent lives during a tumultuous time, Tolstoy creates a perfect composite hero in my view. <br /><br />This is 5 stars for me but a couple things I didn’t like: <br /><br />(1) The randomly interspersed giant sections that struck me as rambling by Tolstoy about history and Napoleon. I don’t know enough about the time to say anything conclusively, but if you bring up “the historians” as a monolith what feels like several hundred times throughout the fantastic story, it seems like you are trying to revise history without the benefit of facts. And he gets extremely philosophical at times in a way I couldn’t follow. The entire second epilogue is this and I just didn’t enjoy it, despite loving the story. <br /><br />(2) At one point the characters describe Sonya as basically not an individual person capable of real feeling. What I like about Russian literature is that it has a way of highlighting the deepest feelings that exist in everyone, and it felt like they tried to explain away a character being hard done by a very sad situation as non sentient <br /><br />I’ll end this review with a quote I liked. There are plenty more in the book but this one I saved as it came at an important inflection point in the story:<br /><br />&gt; We imagine that when we are thrown out of our usual ruts all is lost, but it is only then that what is new and good begins. While there is life there is happiness.",
