    "review": "I really enjoyed The Brothers Karamazov and read that The Idiot was similar. I hoped that The Idiot would build on some of the themes introduced by Aloysha in TBK. The book left me very disappointed as a result. Prince Myshkin felt like a very primitive version of Aloysha which makes sense as the book was written before TBK, but I honestly don’t know if I would’ve picked TBK up quickly after this. It just felt very muted.<br /><br />Myshkin was just a completely neutral element in everything. I can appreciate the value in forgiveness and mercy which I believe is the main point here, but it was very frustrating to see Myshkin only take life as it comes to him. There is no analogue to the side story of Aloysha guiding the group of young kids and all of the beautiful details that come from that simple detour from the main story.<br /><br />It’s possible that Dostoyevsky meant to highlight something here: that it isn’t enough to be meek, forgiving, and kind. You must also have a backbone or these virtues mean little. I am not sure. <br /><br />Additionally, this felt much different from other Dostoyevsky books. It felt disorganized and at times lacking a lot of substance. For me, not an essential read.",
